 - **Recommended surface treatment** — A theme variant's authored recommendation for whether its surfaces are frosted or opaque. It is used only when the user keeps the surface preference at theme default.
 - **Surface preference** — A device-local choice of theme default, frosted, or opaque that is independent of appearance, theme, and accent selections.
 - **Resolved surface treatment** — The effective frosted or opaque treatment produced by applying the surface preference to the active variant's recommendation.
+
+## Cloud vocabulary
+
+- **Cloud account** — The user's connection to one provider, such as their Docker account. Avoid: connection, holder.
+- **Sandbox** — A machine a provider makes so chats can run on it. While it runs it is one of the user's devices, signed in as them, and the chats' agents run on it. Avoid: machine (in the UI), session device.
+- **Provider** — A kind of cloud that makes sandboxes. Two are built into the engine: Docker Sandbox and Boat. Avoid: plugin (providers are not plugins yet).
+- **Host device** — The one device that keeps a cloud account's key and asks the provider for sandboxes on behalf of the user's other devices.
+- **Place** — Where a new chat will run: one of the user's devices, Zeron Cloud, or a provider with a sandbox template. A composer asks a place what agents it offers before any sandbox exists. Avoid: target, location.
+- **Sandbox template** — A saved sandbox that new sandboxes start from. In the code and docs always say "sandbox template", never bare "template", which means a zgx UI template (such as zeron-v2). Inside the Docker Sandbox picker the UI may label them just "Templates". It belongs to the cloud account, and the user picks one each time they start a sandbox chat. What a sandbox has beyond Zeron itself (agents, their logins, tools) comes from its sandbox template or step scripts.
+- **Step** — One stage every sandbox goes through, the same for every provider: create, start, install Zeron, sign in, get the code, setup, sleep, wake, delete. A provider supplies only the steps that need it; Zeron does the rest.
+- **Step script** — The user's own script that runs before, after or instead of a step, for every sandbox, one provider's, or one repo's.
